---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `I have a file that is just bytes written out as ones and
zeros, and somebody swears it is text. Read it back to me.`;

const SampleOutput = `01001000 = 72 = H
01101001 = 105 = i
00100001 = 33 = !`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-7" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 7
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Byte Reader</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code code={Brief} frame="code" lang="txt" title="What they asked for" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-7">Set 7</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>You wrote <code>toDecimal</code> at 2.14. Reuse it &mdash; do not write a second one.</li>
            <li><code>String.fromCharCode(n)</code> turns a number into the character that number stands for. That is the only new tool you need.</li>
            <li>Print all three forms on each line: the bits, the number, the character. The point is that they are the same thing.</li>
            <li>In your requirements list, say which of the three is <b>the data</b> and which two are <b>readings of it</b>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="where-to-start">
          <h2>Where to Start</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Only one-star challenges have this section.</b> If you already know how to
            begin, skip it &mdash; and when you move up to two stars, working out the order
            yourself is part of the job.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>Copy <code>toDecimal</code> from your 2.14 program.</li>
            <li>Convert one byte and print the number. Check it by hand against the place-value table.</li>
            <li>Pass that number to <code>String.fromCharCode</code> and print the character.</li>
            <li>Loop the list, printing all three forms per line.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code code={SampleOutput} frame="terminal" title="Sample Output" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Three bytes, and they spell something. Check 72 against the place-value
            table by hand before you trust the program.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-byte-reader</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
